## Proof

Assume the triangle is acute (so the orthocenter $H$ lies inside $\triangle ABC$ and the configuration is non-degenerate). Set up coordinates with the foot $D$ of the altitude from $B$ as the origin, $AC$ along the $x$-axis, and $BD$ along the $y$-axis:

$$D=(0,0),\quad A=(-a,0),\quad C=(c,0),\quad B=(0,h),\qquad a,c,h>0.$$

**Orthocenter.** The altitude from $A$ is perpendicular to $BC$ (direction $(c,-h)$), so it has direction $(h,c)$ and passes through $(-a,0)$: $(-a+ht,\, ct)$. Meeting the line $BD$ (i.e. $x=0$) gives $t=a/h$, hence

$$H=\Bigl(0,\,\frac{ac}{h}\Bigr).$$

In the acute case $ac<h^{2}$, so $H$ lies between $D$ and $B$.

**The circle $(BCD)$.** Since $\angle BDC=90^{\circ}$, the circumcircle of $BCD$ has $BC$ as a diameter, with equation

$$x^{2}+y^{2}=cx+hy. \tag{1}$$

**The perpendicular bisector of $HD$.** Both $H$ and $D$ lie on the $y$-axis, so $HD$ is vertical and its perpendicular bisector is the horizontal line through the midpoint of $HD$:

$$y=k,\qquad k:=\frac{ac}{2h}. \tag{2}$$

**The points $P,Q$.** Substituting $y=k$ into (1):

$$x^{2}-cx+(k^{2}-hk)=0. \tag{3}$$

Let its roots be $p,q$, so that $P=(p,k)$, $Q=(q,k)$ and

$$p+q=c,\qquad pq=k^{2}-hk=k(k-h). \tag{4}$$

Set $s:=h-k=\dfrac{2h^{2}-ac}{2h}$; then $k+s=h$ and, crucially,

$$pq=k(k-h)=-ks. \tag{5}$$

Since the triangle is acute, $ac<h^{2}<2h^{2}$, so $s>0$ and $k>0$, giving $pq=-ks<0$: **$p$ and $q$ have opposite signs.** $\quad(\star)$

---

**Dot products.** With $P=(p,k)$, $A=(-a,0)$, $B=(0,h)$:

$$\vec{PA}=(-a-p,\,-k),\quad \vec{PB}=(-p,\,h-k)=(-p,\,s),$$

$$\vec{PA}\cdot\vec{PB}=p(a+p)-ks+k^{2}=ap+p^{2}+(k^{2}-hk)=ap+p^{2}+pq=p(a+p+q)=p(a+c),$$

using $p+q=c$ and $pq=k^{2}-hk$. By the identical computation,

$$\vec{PA}\cdot\vec{PB}=p(a+c),\qquad \vec{QA}\cdot\vec{QB}=q(a+c). \tag{6}$$

By $(\star)$ these two dot products have **opposite signs**, so one of $\angle APB,\angle AQB$ is acute and the other obtuse.

---

**Equal cosines in absolute value.** We claim

$$p^{2}\,|QA|^{2}\,|QB|^{2}=q^{2}\,|PA|^{2}\,|PB|^{2}. \tag{7}$$

Indeed $|PA|^{2}=(a+p)^{2}+k^{2}$, $|PB|^{2}=p^{2}+s^{2}$, and similarly with $q$. Define, for $x\neq0$,

$$F(x)=\frac{\bigl[(a+x)^{2}+k^{2}\bigr]\bigl[x^{2}+s^{2}\bigr]}{x^{2}}.$$

Expanding the numerator:

$$\bigl[(a+x)^{2}+k^{2}\bigr]\bigl[x^{2}+s^{2}\bigr]=x^{4}+2ax^{3}+(a^{2}+k^{2}+s^{2})x^{2}+2as^{2}x+(a^{2}+k^{2})s^{2},$$

so

$$F(x)=x^{2}+2ax+(a^{2}+k^{2}+s^{2})+\frac{2as^{2}}{x}+\frac{(a^{2}+k^{2})s^{2}}{x^{2}}.$$

Using $\frac1p-\frac1q=-\frac{p-q}{pq}$ and $\frac1{p^{2}}-\frac1{q^{2}}=-\frac{(p-q)(p+q)}{p^{2}q^{2}}$,

$$F(p)-F(q)=(p-q)\Bigl[(p+q)+2a-\frac{2as^{2}}{pq}-\frac{(a^{2}+k^{2})s^{2}(p+q)}{p^{2}q^{2}}\Bigr].$$

Since $p\neq q$, it suffices to verify the bracket vanishes. Substitute $p+q=c$, $pq=-ks$, $p^{2}q^{2}=k^{2}s^{2}$:

$$c+2a+\frac{2as}{k}-\frac{(a^{2}+k^{2})c}{k^{2}}.$$

Multiplying by $k^{2}$:

$$ck^{2}+2ak^{2}+2ask-(a^{2}+k^{2})c=2ak^{2}+2ask-a^{2}c=2a\,k(k+s)-a^{2}c.$$

Now $k+s=h$ and $k=\frac{ac}{2h}$, so $2a\,k\,h=2a\cdot\frac{ac}{2h}\cdot h=a^{2}c$. The expression equals $a^{2}c-a^{2}c=0$. ✓

Thus $F(p)=F(q)$, which is exactly (7). Dividing (7) by $(a+c)^{2}|PA|^{2}|PB|^{2}|QA|^{2}|QB|^{2}$ and using (6):

$$\frac{(\vec{PA}\cdot\vec{PB})^{2}}{|PA|^{2}|PB|^{2}}=\frac{(\vec{QA}\cdot\vec{QB})^{2}}{|QA|^{2}|QB|^{2}},\qquad\text{i.e.}\quad \cos^{2}\angle APB=\cos^{2}\angle AQB. \tag{8}$$

---

**Conclusion.** From (6) and $(\star)$, $\vec{PA}\cdot\vec{PB}$ and $\vec{QA}\cdot\vec{QB}$ have opposite signs, so

$$\cos\angle APB=-\cos\angle AQB.$$

Both $\angle APB$ and $\angle AQB$ lie strictly between $0^{\circ}$ and $180^{\circ}$, and two such angles with opposite cosines are supplementary. Therefore

$$\boxed{\angle APB+\angle AQB=180^{\circ}}.$$

